  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nElevated, post frontal thunderstorms are expected to develop around \nthe James River Valley later this evening. Timing appears to support \nthe better chances from about 9 pm to midnight for any stronger \nupdrafts. Assuming the models are correct with the moisture around \n800-700mb behind the front, about 1000-1500 J/kg CAPE is expected \nwith about 30-40 knots of effective shear. This may support hail to \nthe size of quarters and with DCAPE about 1000-1500 J/kg some \nstronger gusts of 65 to 70 mph are possible. Of note is that the \nfreezing level is around 14000 feet which will limit the hail threat \na bit.\n\nTuesday will see some mid level moisture and weak elevated \ninstability linger, which should drive scattered showers throughout \nthe day. The better chances will be south of I-90. Amounts will not \nbe very high, but a few locations should get 0.10\" to 0.25\". The \nwidespread cloud cover and scattered showers will limit temperatures \nas well, with highs generally in the mid 70s.\n\nAnother wave will move by on Wednesday, with more cloud cover and \nrain chances near and south of roughly the Missouri River. With \ncloud cover and somewhat cooler low to mid level temperatures highs \nwill again be below normal, ranging from 75 to 80 near and north of \nI-90 to the mid 70s south.\n\nA warming trend will set up for the weekend as west northwest flow \naloft becomes more westerly. Temperatures should climb into9 the 80s \non Thursday, then mid 80s to lower 90s Friday and Saturday with some \nquestion marks for Sunday as a wave could bring slightly cooler \nconditions. While there is no rain in the forecast during this time, \neach of the models generates weak waves, with little to no agreement \non when. So, as the weekend approaches, there may be a couple of \nbrief periods when showers and thunderstorms develop.",
